{% import 'chrome/macros.html.j2' as c %}
<section>
  <h2>The Proposed Design</h2>

  <p>Not a ladder of quality. Three genuinely different mechanisms, each honest about what it
  is, selected by asking the browser what it supports rather than which browser it claims to
  be.</p>

  {{ c.figure('assets/01-tiers.svg', 3,
     'Which mechanism applies, decided by capability rather than by browser name. Cyan marks
      the paths where the application can report real progress; purple marks the path where it
      cannot see the transfer at all. Checking for the capability rather than the browser
      matters because browser identity strings are trivially altered and change meaning
      between releases.') }}

  <h3>Tier 1 — Managed folder</h3>
  <p>Available on Chromium desktop, and on Android with the defects noted earlier. The
  application streams into a folder the user chooses. Real progress, resume to the exact byte,
  a real folder tree, and no quota ceiling. Each chunk is signed separately, so no long-lived
  link ever exists.</p>

  <h3>Tier 2 — Staged download</h3>
  <p>Available on Chromium and Firefox, and attempted but not claimed on Safari. The
  application fetches with streaming for real progress and completion, stages into private
  origin storage, and exports at the end. Resume survives a reload because the staged bytes
  persist. The costs are that files arrive flat, the total is bounded by quota, and the file
  is briefly on disk twice.</p>

  <h3>Tier 3 — Browser handoff</h3>
  <p>Available everywhere, and shipped today. A signed link with a disposition header, handed
  to the browser's own download manager. No progress and no completion signal — but no memory
  ceiling either, and on Android the operating system can continue the transfer after the
  browser exits. This remains the only viable path for genuinely large jobs outside
  Chromium.</p>

  <h3>Tier 0 — Command-line handoff</h3>
  <p>Already shipped. For anything approaching a terabyte, a purpose-built transfer tool beats
  any browser: it survives restarts, needs no window open, and retries indefinitely.</p>

  {{ c.note('Parity is not achievable, and should not be promised',
     'Progress and completion can be equalised across every target, because streaming reads
      are universal. The destination cannot be: only one engine family can write a folder tree,
      and only the handoff path escapes a quota ceiling.') }}

  <h3>A decision this settles: keep the single-file architecture</h3>

  <p>Bucketer ships as one self-contained HTML file that can be opened directly, audited, and
  dropped anywhere. The capability this forgoes is a background transfer service, which
  requires shipping a second script file alongside it. The investigation says do not:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>Safari and iPhone gain nothing from it — they support neither that service nor the
    alternative it would enable.</li>
    <li>On Firefox the equivalent technique has an open defect in which the transfer halts
    part-way while the browser reports success on a <em>truncated</em> file. Silent corruption
    is the worst possible failure for a tool people use to move their data.</li>
    <li>Chromium already has a better option that needs no second file at all.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>It buys nothing on the one platform it would help, and fixes nothing on the platforms it
  would not.</p>
</section>
